  "numerical_failure": {
    "category": "correctness",
    "affected_api": "sklearn.linear_model._base.LinearClassifierMixin._predict_proba_lr",
    "observed_behavior": "When decision_function returns large negative values for all classes, all probabilities underflow to zero in floating point arithmetic; then normalization divides by zero and predict_proba() returns NaN values for some inputs, along with a RuntimeWarning: invalid value encountered in divide.",
    "expected_behavior": "predict_proba() returns a valid categorical probability distribution over the classes; in the fixed behavior described in the whatsnew, it assigns equal probability for each class when this occurs.",
    "manifestation": "warning",
    "trigger_conditions": [
      "Linear models with log loss where training produces model with large coefficients",
      "For a single input, decision_function(X) returns large negative values for every class (so exp(decision_function) underflows to 0 for all classes)",
      "During multiclass probability normalization in _predict_proba_lr, prob.sum(axis=1) becomes zero, leading to prob /= prob.sum(...) division"
    ],
    "input_output_examples": [
      {
        "input": "A single input X for which decision_function(X) is large negative for every class (details not provided in the passages)",
        "buggy_output": "predict_proba() contains NaNs for some inputs; a RuntimeWarning occurs: invalid value encountered in divide at sklearn/linear_model/_base.py:440 (divide prob by prob.sum).",
        "expected_output": "A valid categorical probability distribution; the fix assigns equal probability for each class.",
        "value_kind": "array",
        "source": "Issue #17978 description and error trace; PR/whatsnew text describing the fix"
      }
    ],
    "oracle_clues": [
      "Bug is triggered when decision_function() returns large negative values for every class for a single input, making exp(-1000) = 0 in floating point arithmetic (example given).",
      "Normalization in predict_proba_lr divides by zero when all probabilities are zero, producing NaNs.",
      "RuntimeWarning observed: \"invalid value encountered in divide\" in sklearn/linear_model/_base.py at line 440.",
      "Expected behavior (fix): assigns equal probability for each class when probabilities become all zeros."
    ]
  },
  "numerical_characteristics": {
    "dtype": null,
    "precision": null,
    "magnitude": null,
    "tolerance": null,
    "category_specific_evidence": [
      "predict_proba() can return NaNs when decision_function() returns large negative values for every class for a single input, causing normalization in predict_proba_lr() to divide by zero and return NaNs (issue #17978).",
      "In _predict_proba_lr, the fix addresses the case where exp(-1000)=0 in floating point arithmetic, which can make all probabilities zero so normalization by prob.sum(axis=1) leads to NaN/inf (PR #33168 description).",
      "The code change computes prob_sum = prob.sum(axis=1), checks all_zero = prob_sum == 0, and when any row has prob_sum==0 sets prob[all_zero,:] = 1 and prob_sum[all_zero] = prob.shape[1] (n_classes) before dividing, to produce uniform probabilities instead of NaN/inf (diff).",
      "Whatsnew entry: large negative values of decision_function could lead to NaN values in multiclass settings; fix assigns equal probability for each class (doc/whats_new).",
      "Test is added specifically to validate that _predict_proba_lr deals with large values for LinearClassifierMixin (test_diff / test description)."
    ],
    "reproduction_risks": [
      "Evidence mentions exp(-1000)=0 as an example of large negative magnitude but does not specify exact feature inputs or coefficients; reproductions must ensure decision_function is large negative for every class so that probabilities become all zero (prob_sum==0).",
      "The issue context is for SGDClassifier with log loss and default alpha, but the passages do not provide the exact parameter values or dataset needed to recreate the specific coefficient regime.",
      "Tolerance/precision requirements are not stated; reproductions that rely on approximate numerical equality may be under/over-specified since only NaN/inf and normalization-by-zero behavior are described."
    ]
  },
